Z||000018||LZeitgeist||09-28-2001||10:25 PM||||To me, an automatic in a Fiero negates a great deal of why a Fiero feels right to me. <P>Just my personal preference... <BR><P>------------------<BR>Patrick W. Heinske  --  LZeitgeist@aol.com<BR>1988 Red Fiero Formula<BR>- 1st Place - Stock Coupe - FOCOSEVA 2000<BR>- 3rd Place - Stock Formula - FOCOA Nat'l 2001<BR>- Class Winner - 1982 to Present - 2001 Tarheel Tigers All-Pontiac Show||||reg||1
Z||000019||ChadMan||09-28-2001||11:04 PM||||I was a diehard manual guy until I drove my father-in-law's 86 SE V6 automatic from Columbus to Phoenix.<P>It's like this:  A stick shift Fiero is a sports car.  An automatic Fiero is a grand touring car.<P>I wish I had one of each.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000020||Cheever3000||09-28-2001||11:15 PM||||Even in Houston traffic it's rare when I wished I had an automatic.<P>My vote is for manual.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000021||theogre||09-28-2001||11:32 PM||||I've driven more than a few manual transmission..... the "fun" wears off really fast when you have to drive in urban areas or any place else where there's allot of heavy traffic and stop lights. Add to that all the problems such driving adds to reliability... broken pressure plates, blown hydraulics, fried disks, etc. I've had way more than enough of that crap.<P>While a 5 speed may have an edge in highway fuel economy, automatics beat them in city driving because they shift more consistantly than you can. Even the highway mileage advantage is questionable when you throw a properly installed AOD tranny in the mix. (I'm currently averaging in the 26mpg range for mixed driving. I've gotten 32mpg when it's all highway. 87 4 cyl/TH125)<P>stick is not inherantly faster off the line than automatic. The torque multiply of the torque converter means more power goes into the tranny than a simple clutch. According to data posted in another thread, one book about the TH125 specifies as high as 1.95:1 TC multiply. So if a 4 cyl makes 134poundfeet peek then at peek multiply you've got 261.3poundfeet pumping into the auto tranny. But then once you are cruising the TC locks and eliminates nearly all the power loss of older non TC locking automatics.<P>You'd have to pay me a considerable amount of money to get me to drive manual tranny again.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000022||Tigger||09-29-2001||01:13 AM||||Well, I prefer to drive manuals since 98% of people who have been abducted by Aliens drove cars with automatic transmissions...<P>Shift Yourself   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000023||fierogtracer||09-29-2001||01:28 AM||||Auto..<P>I have had 2 manual fieros and 2 autos the manual was my favorite untill I had a little work done on the trans. Now the auto preforms far better than any of my manuals.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000024||Joe Torma||09-29-2001||02:06 AM||||Automatics... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_zz.gif"><P>4 speeds...mmm mmm good.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig1.gif">  <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR> <A HREF="http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm</A>||||reg||1
Z||000025||webbee||09-29-2001||02:43 AM||||I've been driving for 40+ years and I'll take a stick anytime. Even on the 405, in rush, I control much better with a stick. I think autos are for lazy boys. Unfortunately my Fiero is an auto. But no clutch problems.||||reg||1
